-- #1182 — RE-POSED ONTO THE CROSS-MODULE AXIS (2026-08-15). Two DIFFERENT interfaces,
-- declared in two DIFFERENT modules, each declare a method named `m`. Which impl runs is
-- decided by the DECLARATION ORDER OF THE TWO `impl` BLOCKS in this file. Swapping them
-- (control.mdk) changes no program meaning and changes the answer.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY IT MOVED. The previous shape put both interfaces in THIS file. The Q1 unit
-- (Stage B / Phase 4b) now rejects that on the declaration (R-DUPLICATE-IFACE-METHOD), so
-- the old fixture no longer reaches the defect it pins — it stopped compiling. #1182 is
-- NOT fixed by Q1: Q1 covers only the intra-module half, and `duplicateErrors` runs inside
-- `buildErrors`, which has no import table at all, so nothing in it can see two interfaces
-- that meet only through an `import`. This file is that surviving half.
--
-- ⭐ THIS POSING IS STRICTLY STRONGER THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES, and the difference is worth
-- reading before judging a red. The old single-file fixture had to disclaim its own value
-- ("does not assert that 1 is wrong and 2 is right") because BOTH `A1.m` and `A2.m` were in
-- scope and which one the occurrence denoted was #1182's genuinely open question. Here the
-- entry imports the method name from a1 ONLY (`import a1.{A1, m}`) and imports a2 for its
-- INTERFACE NAME ONLY. So the occurrence of `m` below has exactly one binding — A1's — and
-- printing 2 means the program ran the body of a method belonging to an interface this
-- occurrence never named. THAT IS WRONG UNDER EVERY READING, with no name-resolution
-- question left to settle.
--
-- HAND-DERIVED BEFORE ANY RUN (the engine is the thing under suspicion, so its output is
-- not the oracle here):
--   * `m` binds to A1's method; the goal is `A1 (Q Int Bool Int)`.
--   * CORRECT, interface-scoped selection: the candidate set holds only
--     `impl A1 (Q Int y z)` -> the answer is 1, and it is INVARIANT under impl-block order.
--   * ACTUAL: `matchingEntries` selects by bare METHOD-NAME membership
--     (`contains name methodNames`), so `impl A2 (Q x Bool z)` joins the same candidate
--     set. `Q Int y z` and `Q x Bool z` are ⊑-incomparable, so `pickMostSpecificEntry` has
--     no minimum and commits to the HEAD of the list -> the first-declared impl wins ->
--     this file prints 2 and control.mdk prints 1.
-- Then measured on this branch's binary, and it agreed with the derivation:
--   `check --json main.mdk` -> 3 files, all `"diagnostics":[]`, exit 0 (SILENT)
--   `run main.mdk`          -> 2, exit 0
--   `build main.mdk` + exec -> 2, exit 0 (the native engine AGREES — not an eval artifact)
--   `run control.mdk`       -> 1, exit 0
--
-- ⇒ EVERY FIX DIRECTION DRAINS THIS ROW CLEANLY, which is why `main` is posed on the
-- ordering that yields the WRONG value rather than the right one: scoping the candidate set
-- by interface makes this file print 1, the pinned stdout moves, and the control (which
-- asserts 1) still passes. No misleading CONTROL-BROKE, unlike the shape this replaced.
import a1.{A1, m}
import a2.{A2}

data Q a b c = Q a b c

impl A2 (Q x Bool z) where
  m _ = 2

impl A1 (Q Int y z) where
  m _ = 1

main = println (m (Q 1 True 7))
